================================================================================
SINDRI
================================================================================

A marketplace for makers. Buy and sell code, design, and tech services with
escrow protection — the seller doesn't get paid until the buyer confirms the
work, or a dispute window passes.

Sindri is free and open-source software.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
QUICK START
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

You need: Go 1.22+, Node 20+, PostgreSQL 15+, and make.

  1. Install tools, create .env, verify the DB is reachable
       make setup

  2. Apply migrations to both dev and test databases
       make migrate

  3. Run the API (backend)
       make run

  4. In a second terminal, run the frontend
       make web-dev

  5. Optional: seed 1000 users + products + wallets for local testing
       go run ./cmd/seed

Backend listens on http://localhost:8080
Frontend dev server on http://localhost:5173 (Vite default), proxied to backend.

Log in with one of the seeded users:

  pitrucci@example.com    hunter2hunter2
  romeo@example.com       hunter2hunter2
  james@example.com       hunter2hunter2

...or any sindri_NNNN@example.com created by the seed, same password.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROJECT LAYOUT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

cmd/
  api/            HTTP server entrypoint
  worker/         Auto-release worker (runs every 5 min)
  admin-cli/      Promote a user to admin
  seed/           Generate fake users, products, wallets for local testing

internal/
  admin/          Admin operations (users, warnings, tickets, settings)
  auth/           JWT manager, middleware, DB-backed user checker
  category/       Category tree (two levels: parent + child)
  chat/           Encrypted chat, WebSocket hub, attachments
  crypto/         AES-256-GCM for chat at rest
  currency/       Currency metadata (codes, symbols, decimal digits)
  db/             Connection pool + sqlc-generated query package
  httpapi/        HTTP handlers and router
  notification/   In-app notifications (DB + WebSocket push)
  order/          Orders, invoices, escrow, disputes
  product/        Product listings, images, search
  profile/        Public user profiles, geek stats
  review/         Buyer-to-seller reviews
  settings/       Platform settings (commission, escrow days, KYC gates)
  user/           Registration, auth, search
  wallet/         Balances, deposits, ledger
  withdrawal/     Withdrawal requests + admin review

migrations/       Goose SQL migrations

web/              Vue 3 + TypeScript frontend
  public/         Static assets (logo.svg, logo-badge.svg, ...)
  src/
    components/   Reusable Vue components
    composables/  Composition API helpers (e.g. useBrand)
    config/       Runtime config (brand, API URLs)
    lib/          Utilities (money formatting, WS client)
    stores/       Pinia stores (auth, chat, notifications, panel)
    views/        Page-level Vue components


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
COMMANDS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

All commands live in the Makefile. Run make (or make help) for the full list.

  make setup                    One-time: install tools, copy .env, verify DB
  make run                      Run the API server
  make worker                   Run the auto-release worker
  make web-dev                  Run the frontend dev server
  make test                     Run all tests against DATABASE_URL_TEST
  make test-verbose             Same, with -v -race
  make migrate                  Apply pending migrations to dev + test DB
  make migrate-create NAME=foo  Create a new migration
  make migrate-down             Roll back the last migration
  make sqlc                     Regenerate type-safe Go from SQL
  make build                    go build ./...
  make lint                     go vet ./...
  make fmt                      gofmt -w .
  make tidy                     go mod tidy
  make promote EMAIL=x@y.z      Promote a user to admin


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ENVIRONMENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Copy .env.example to .env and fill in:

  DATABASE_URL           required    Postgres URL for the dev database
  DATABASE_URL_TEST      required    Postgres URL for the test database
  APP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS    required    Comma-separated WS origin allowlist
                                     e.g. http://localhost:5173
  JWT_SECRET             prod only   Signs JWTs; dev fallback logs a warning
  CHAT_ENCRYPTION_KEY    prod only   Base64-encoded 32 bytes for AES-256-GCM
  HTTP_PORT              optional    API port (default 8080)
  UPLOADS_DIR            optional    Where uploaded files go (default ./uploads)
  COOKIE_SECURE          optional    Force secure cookies (auto true in prod)
  VITE_API_URL           required    Backend URL the Vite dev proxy targets
  VITE_APP_BRAND_NAME    optional    Brand name in the topbar (default sindri)

make test deliberately overrides DATABASE_URL to point at DATABASE_URL_TEST,
so tests never touch your dev database.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BRAND NAME AND LOGO
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The brand name is runtime config, not code and not i18n. To rebrand:

  1. Change VITE_APP_BRAND_NAME in web/.env (or .env.local for a personal
     override).
  2. Restart the dev server — the topbar picks up the new name automatically.
  3. If the accent color changes too, update --accent and --accent-hover in
     the theme CSS. The inline >_ mark inherits currentColor, so it recolors
     on its own.
  4. If you also want to change the mark itself (currently the >_ shell
     glyph), replace web/public/logo.svg (transparent, currentColor) and
     web/public/logo-badge.svg (background baked in, used as the favicon).

The mark >_ was chosen because it reads as "shell / coding / terminal" and is
name-agnostic — it survives a rename without redesign. The name Sindri comes
from Norse mythology: the master smith who forged Mjolnir and Draupnir.

See BRAND.md for the full contract.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TESTING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  All tests, against DATABASE_URL_TEST:
    make test

  Just one package:
    DATABASE_URL=postgres://.../sindri_test go test ./internal/admin/... -v

  A single test:
    DATABASE_URL=postgres://.../sindri_test go test ./internal/admin/... -run TestSetKYC -v

Tests follow the conventions in DECISIONS.md:

  - DB cleanup before any assert (ADR-014) — register t.Cleanup immediately
    after a resource ID exists, before any t.Fatal.

  - Integration tests, no browser E2E (ADR-013) — httptest.Server against a
    real DB catches most bugs at a fraction of the cost.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ARCHITECTURE DECISIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DECISIONS.md records every policy-level decision — how escrow works, why the
withdrawal flow is admin-reviewed, why passwords are Argon2id, why chat is not
E2EE, and so on. Read it before changing anything that looks like a rule.

New decisions get appended as ADR-NNN. Don't rewrite history.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SEED DATA
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

For local development and demos, cmd/seed populates the database with 1000
fake users, each with 10–20 products and a funded wallet. Three "named"
accounts always exist:

  pitrucci@example.com
  romeo@example.com
  james@example.com

All with password hunter2hunter2. Generated users follow the pattern
sindri_NNNN@example.com, same password.

  Full seed (1000 users):
    go run ./cmd/seed

  Smaller run:
    go run ./cmd/seed -users 100 -products-min 3 -products-max 8

  Re-create only the three named accounts (useful after a DB wipe):
    go run ./cmd/seed -users 0

  Clean up all sindri_* users (named users are kept):
    go run ./cmd/seed -clean

The seed is idempotent: re-running it will reset the named users' passwords
and skip the generated ones that already exist.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONTRIBUTING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Sindri is open-source and contributions are welcome.

  - Fork the repository.
  - Create a branch for your change.
  - Run make test and make lint before opening a pull request.
  - Keep commits focused; one logical change per commit.
  - Read DECISIONS.md before changing anything that looks like policy.

For larger changes, open an issue first to discuss the approach.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STATUS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

MVP, single-instance deployment. See DECISIONS.md for what that implies
(no horizontal scaling, in-memory WebSocket hub, per-instance rate limits).

Known gaps:

  - KYC is partially integrated — admin override exists, but there is no
    user-facing submission flow. See ADR-016.

  - No refresh tokens. JWT lifetime is 24h; the DB check on every request
    means a disabled user loses access immediately, but a demoted admin
    keeps their non-admin session for up to 24h of authenticated browsing.


